        "content": "## Summary\n\n**Target Fact:** `F:ml430-int-modeq-neg-f649f6c5`  \n**Formal Statement:** `∀ {n a b : ℤ}, a ≡ b [ZMOD n] → -a ≡ -b [ZMOD n]`\n\n**Structural Shape:** This is a modular equivalence preservation theorem showing that negation respects the ModEq relation. It takes a hypothesis `a ≡ b [ZMOD n]` and proves the conclusion `-a ≡ -b [ZMOD n]`. The goal has universal quantifiers over three integers (n, a, b) with an implication structure.\n\n**Dependencies:** The fact has no dependencies (`depends_on: []`) and unlocks nothing (`would_unlock: []`), making it a leaf node in the dependency graph.\n\n**Available Premises:** The Int prelude contains 394 theorems total. No theorems matching `*ModEq*` exist in the kernel (the glob failed to match), but 26 theorems about negation exist, including `Int.modEq_neg_modulus` and `Int.modEq_of_neg_modulus` which handle negating the modulus rather than the arguments.\n\n**Similar Eligible Facts:** The frontier contains 98 eligible facts, including several related ModEq theorems:\n- `F:ml430-int-neg-modeq-neg-30d98479`: The biconditional version `∀ {n a b : ℤ}, -a ≡ -b [ZMOD n] ↔ a ≡ b [ZMOD n]`\n- `F:ml430-int-modeq-add-left-6e17c69a`: Addition preservation\n- Multiple other ModEq congruence facts\n\n**Existing Operations:** No registered operations target this fact.",
